"/></svg><div class="progress-circle-label" id="progressLabel">0%</div></div><nav class="topnav"><div class="topnav-inner"><a href="https://sustainsys.co.uk" class="nav-logo"><img src="../../assets/logo_only.png" alt="SustainSys" class="nav-logo-img"><div class="nav-logo-text">Sustain<span>Sys</span></div></a><div class="nav-divider"></div><div class="nav-title">Module 10 — RAG Evaluation</div><span class="nav-badge">Associate</span><a href="index.html" class="nav-home-link">← Course Home</a></div></nav><aside class="sidebar"><div class="sidebar-course-title">AI Practitioner Associate</div><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 10: RAG Pipelines</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">10.1</span> What is RAG?</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">10.2</span> Document Chunking</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">10.3</span> Embeddings &amp; Vectors</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">10.4</span> Building RAG</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson5" class="active"><span class="sidebar-num">10.5</span> RAG Evaluation</a></li></ul></aside><button class="sidebar-toggle">☰</button><main class="main"><div class="container"><section class="hero"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="index.html">Associate</a><span class="sep">›</span><span>RAG Evaluation</span></div><span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 10 · Lesson 5</span><h1>RAG Evaluation and Improvement</h1><p class="hero-sub">Learn how to evaluate RAG system quality and identify bottlenecks. Implement metrics for retrieval quality, generation quality, and end-to-end performance. Discover optimization strategies for improving answer quality and system efficiency in production.</p></section><div class="objectives"><h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Understand metrics for evaluating RAG system quality</li><li>Implement retrieval evaluation metrics like precision and recall</li><li>Evaluate generation quality using LLM-based assessment</li><li>Conduct end-to-end evaluation with ground truth data</li><li>Identify bottlenecks in RAG pipelines</li><li>Implement A/B testing for RAG improvements</li><li>Optimize RAG systems for your specific use case</li><li>Monitor and maintain RAG quality in production</li></ul></div><section class="section" id="evaluation-framework"><span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span><h2>RAG Evaluation Framework</h2><p>RAG systems have multiple quality dimensions. You can't evaluate them with a single metric. Instead, you need a framework measuring retrieval quality, generation quality, and end-to-end performance. Understanding where your system excels and where it fails is crucial for targeted improvement.</p><div class="key-concept"><strong>Core Insight:</strong> RAG quality depends on retrieval. If the right documents aren't retrieved, even a perfect LLM can't answer correctly. Invest in retrieval evaluation first.</div><svg viewBox="0 0 900 280" style="width:100%; max-width:800px; margin:20px auto; display:block;"><defs><marker id="arrowhead" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="7" refX="9" refY="3.5" orient="auto"><polygon points="0 0, 10 3.5, 0 7" fill="#333"/></marker></defs><g><rect x="20" y="40" width="140" height="100" fill="#e8f4f8" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" rx="4"/><text x="90" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">Question</text><text x="90" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Dataset with</text><text x="90" y="115" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">ground truth</text><path d="M 160 90 L 210 90" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/><rect x="210" y="40" width="140" height="100" fill="#fff4e6" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" rx="4"/><text x="280" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">Retrieval</text><text x="280" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Evaluate:</text><text x="280" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Precision, Recall</text><text x="280" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">MRR, NDCG</text><path d="M 350 90 L 400 90" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/><rect x="400" y="40" width="140" height="100" fill="#e8f8e8" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" rx="4"/><text x="470" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">Generation</text><text x="470" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Evaluate:</text><text x="470" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Correctness,</text><text x="470" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Relevance, LLM</text><path d="M 540 90 L 590 90" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrowhead)"/><rect x="590" y="40" width="140" height="100" fill="#f4e8f8" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2" rx="4"/><text x="660" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="bold">End-to-End</text><text x="660" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Full question to</text><text x="660" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">answer evaluation</text><text x="660" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">with ground truth</text><text x="450" y="180" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="bold">RAG Evaluation Pipeline</text></g></svg></section><section class="section" id="retrieval-metrics"><span class="section-label">Metrics</span><h2>Retrieval Quality Metrics</h2><p>Retrieval is the foundation of RAG. If you don't retrieve the right documents, generation quality suffers. Key retrieval metrics help identify when retrieval is the bottleneck.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Evaluate retrieval quality</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn.metrics</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">precision_score</span>, <span class="fn">recall_score</span>

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">evaluate_retrieval</span>(<span class="va">retrieved_docs</span>, <span class="va">relevant_docs</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Evaluate retrieval precision and recall."""</span>
    <span class="va">retrieved_ids</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">set</span>([<span class="va">d</span>.<span class="va">id</span> <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">d</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">retrieved_docs</span>])
    <span class="va">relevant_ids</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">set</span>([<span class="va">d</span>.<span class="va">id</span> <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">d</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">relevant_docs</span>])
    
    <span class="va">true_positives</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">retrieved_ids</span> <span class="op">&</span> <span class="va">relevant_ids</span>)
    
    <span class="cm"># Precision: of retrieved, how many are relevant?</span>
    <span class="va">precision</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">true_positives</span> <span class="op">/</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">retrieved_ids</span>) <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">retrieved_ids</span> <span class="kw">else</span> <span class="nm">0</span>
    
    <span class="cm"># Recall: of relevant, how many did we retrieve?</span>
    <span class="va">recall</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">true_positives</span> <span class="op">/</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">relevant_ids</span>) <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">relevant_ids</span> <span class="kw">else</span> <span class="nm">0</span>
    
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Precision: {precision:.2f}"</span>)
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Recall: {recall:.2f}"</span>)</code></pre><p><strong>Precision:</strong> Of the documents you retrieved, how many were actually relevant? High precision means few false positives. Importance: LLM can only work with retrieved documents.</p><p><strong>Recall:</strong> Of all relevant documents, how many did you retrieve? High recall means you didn't miss important information. Importance: Missing documents means missing information for answers.</p><p><strong>Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR):</strong> Measures position of first relevant document. Higher is better.</p><p><strong>NDCG (Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain):</strong> Considers ranking order. Penalizes missing relevant documents more when they should appear early.</p></section><section class="section" id="generation-metrics"><span class="section-label">Evaluation</span><h2>Generation Quality Evaluation</h2><p>Even with perfect retrieval, generation can fail. LLM-based evaluation uses another LLM to assess answer quality across multiple dimensions.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Evaluate generation quality with LLM</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">langchain.chat_models</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">ChatOpenAI</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">langchain.prompts</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">PromptTemplate</span>

<span class="va">evaluator_llm</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">ChatOpenAI</span>(<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"gpt-4"</span>)

<span class="va">evaluation_prompt</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"""Evaluate the answer to this question.

Question: {question}
Context: {context}
Answer: {answer}
Ground Truth: {ground_truth}

Rate on scale 1-5:
1. Correctness (is it factually correct?)
2. Relevance (does it address the question?)
3. Completeness (does it cover all aspects?)

Provide a score and brief explanation."""</span>

<span class="va">prompt</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">PromptTemplate</span>(<span class="va">template</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">evaluation_prompt</span>, <span class="va">input_variables</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">"question"</span>, <span class="st">"context"</span>, <span class="st">"answer"</span>, <span class="st">"ground_truth"</span>])

<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">evaluator_llm</span>.<span class="fn">predict</span>(<span class="va">question</span><span class="op">=</span>..., <span class="va">context</span><span class="op">=</span>..., <span class="va">answer</span><span class="op">=</span>..., <span class="va">ground_truth</span><span class="op">=</span>...)</code></pre></section><section class="section" id="optimization"><span class="section-label">Improvement</span><h2>Optimization Strategies</h2><p>Once you've identified evaluation metrics, use them to guide optimization. Common improvement strategies:</p><div class="subsection"><h3>Improve Retrieval</h3><p>If retrieval metrics are low, focus here first. Try:</p><ul><li>Better embedding model (OpenAI vs. open-source)</li><li>Different chunk size or overlap</li><li>Hybrid search (combine keyword + semantic)</li><li>Re-ranking retrieved documents with a cross-encoder</li></ul><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Re-ranking retrieved documents</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">langchain.retrievers</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">ContextualCompressionRetriever</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">langchain.retrievers.document_compressors</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">CrossEncoderReranker</span>

<span class="va">compressor</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">CrossEncoderReranker</span>()
<span class="va">compression_retriever</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">ContextualCompressionRetriever</span>(
    <span class="va">base_compressor</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">compressor</span>,
    <span class="va">base_retriever</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">vector_store</span>.<span class="fn">as_retriever</span>()
)

<span class="cm"># Re-ranked results more relevant</span>
<span class="va">docs</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">compression_retriever</span>.<span class="fn">get_relevant_documents</span>(<span class="st">"return policy"</span>)</code></pre></div><div class="subsection"><h3>Improve Prompting</h3><p>If retrieval is good but generation is poor, optimize prompts. Try:</p><ul><li>Better system prompts with domain context</li><li>Few-shot examples of good answers</li><li>Chain of Thought prompting for complex reasoning</li><li>Prompt templates that structure output</li></ul></div><div class="subsection"><h3>Document Quality</h3><p>Sometimes the problem is the knowledge base itself. Try:</p><ul><li>Remove outdated or conflicting documents</li><li>Add domain expertise in accessible format</li><li>Improve document organization and structure</li><li>Add metadata to guide retrieval</li></ul></div></section><section class="section" id="continuous-improvement"><span class="section-label">Production</span><h2>Continuous Improvement in Production</h2><p>RAG systems degrade over time as documents change, user needs evolve, and edge cases emerge. Implement continuous monitoring and improvement:</p><ul><li><strong>User Feedback:</strong> Collect thumbs up/down on answers. Track which questions get negative feedback.</li><li><strong>Logging:</strong> Log all queries and retrieved documents. Analyze to identify failure patterns.</li><li><strong>Regular Evaluation:</strong> Monthly evaluation on a stable test set to catch quality drift.</li><li><strong>Document Updates:</strong> Automatically refresh knowledge base from source systems.</li><li><strong>A/B Testing:</strong> Test new embedding models, chunk sizes, or prompts with real users.</li></ul></section><section class="section" id="human-eval"><span class="section-label">Evaluation</span><h2>Human Evaluation and User Feedback</h2><p>Automated metrics reveal patterns but miss nuance. Humans understand context, recognize when answers are incomplete, and catch edge cases. Build human evaluation into your workflow.</p><h3>Structured Human Evaluation</h3><p>Create evaluation rubrics to make human judgment systematic and reproducible. Instead of vague "is this good?", ask specific questions on a scale.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Evaluation rubric</span>
<span class="va">rubric</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">"correctness"</span>: {
        <span class="st">"description"</span>: <span class="st">"Is the answer factually correct?"</span>,
        <span class="st">"scale"</span>: <span class="st">"1=completely wrong, 5=completely correct"</span>,
    },
    <span class="st">"completeness"</span>: {
        <span class="st">"description"</span>: <span class="st">"Does the answer address all aspects of the question?"</span>,
        <span class="st">"scale"</span>: <span class="st">"1=missing key info, 5=comprehensive"</span>,
    },
    <span class="st">"relevance"</span>: {
        <span class="st">"description"</span>: <span class="st">"Is the answer relevant or does it drift to unrelated topics?"</span>,
        <span class="st">"scale"</span>: <span class="st">"1=completely irrelevant, 5=perfectly focused"</span>,
    },
    <span class="st">"clarity"</span>: {
        <span class="st">"description"</span>: <span class="st">"Is the answer clear and well-organized?"</span>,
        <span class="st">"scale"</span>: <span class="st">"1=confusing, 5=crystal clear"</span>,
    }
}

<span class="cm"># Collect evaluations</span>
<span class="va">evaluations</span> <span class="op">=</span> []
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">question</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">test_set</span>:
    <span class="va">answer</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">rag_chain</span>.<span class="fn">run</span>(<span class="va">question</span>)
    <span class="cm"># Human evaluates on rubric</span>
    <span class="va">eval_result</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
        <span class="st">"question"</span>: <span class="va">question</span>,
        <span class="st">"answer"</span>: <span class="va">answer</span>,
        <span class="st">"scores"</span>: {
            <span class="st">"correctness"</span>: <span class="nm">4</span>,  <span class="cm"># Human input</span>
            <span class="st">"completeness"</span>: <span class="nm">3</span>,
            <span class="st">"relevance"</span>: <span class="nm">5</span>,
            <span class="st">"clarity"</span>: <span class="nm">4</span>
        }
    }
    <span class="va">evaluations</span>.<span class="fn">append</span>(<span class="va">eval_result</span>)

<span class="cm"># Aggregate scores</span>
<span class="va">avg_scores</span> <span class="op">=</span> {}
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">metric</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">rubric</span>.<span class="fn">keys</span>():
    <span class="va">scores</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="va">e</span>[<span class="st">"scores"</span>][<span class="va">metric</span>] <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">e</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">evaluations</span>]
    <span class="va">avg_scores</span>[<span class="va">metric</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">sum</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>) <span class="op">/</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>)

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Average scores:"</span>)
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">metric</span>, <span class="va">score</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">avg_scores</span>.<span class="fn">items</span>():
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"  {metric}: {score:.1f}/5"</span>)</code></pre><h3>Collecting In-Application Feedback</h3><p>The best signal comes from real users. Add simple thumbs-up/thumbs-down buttons after each answer. Track which answers get negative feedback to identify patterns and failures.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Log answer for feedback collection</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">log_interaction</span>(<span class="va">question</span>, <span class="va">answer</span>, <span class="va">retrieved_docs</span>, <span class="va">user_feedback</span>=<span class="kw">None</span>):
    <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">json</span>
    <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">datetime</span>

    <span class="va">log_entry</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
        <span class="st">"timestamp"</span>: <span class="fn">datetime</span>.<span class="fn">datetime</span>.<span class="fn">now</span>().<span class="fn">isoformat</span>(),
        <span class="st">"question"</span>: <span class="va">question</span>,
        <span class="st">"answer"</span>: <span class="va">answer</span>,
        <span class="st">"num_retrieved"</span>: <span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">retrieved_docs</span>),
        <span class="st">"retrieved_sources"</span>: [<span class="va">d</span>.<span class="va">metadata</span>[<span class="st">"source"</span>] <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">d</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">retrieved_docs</span>],
        <span class="st">"user_feedback"</span>: <span class="va">user_feedback</span>  <span class="cm"># thumbs_up, thumbs_down, skip</span>
    }

    <span class="cm"># Append to log file</span>
    <span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"rag_interactions.jsonl"</span>, <span class="st">"a"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
        <span class="va">f</span>.<span class="fn">write</span>(<span class="fn">json</span>.<span class="fn">dumps</span>(<span class="va">log_entry</span>) <span class="op">+</span> <span class="st">"\n"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Analyze feedback patterns</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">analyze_feedback</span>():
    <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">json</span>
    <span class="va">negative_feedback_count</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">0</span>
    <span class="va">failing_sources</span> <span class="op">=</span> {}

    <span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="st">"rag_interactions.jsonl"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
        <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">line</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">f</span>:
            <span class="va">entry</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">json</span>.<span class="fn">loads</span>(<span class="va">line</span>)
            <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">entry</span>[<span class="st">"user_feedback"</span>] <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">"thumbs_down"</span>:
                <span class="va">negative_feedback_count</span> <span class="op">+=</span> <span class="nm">1</span>
                <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">source</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">entry</span>[<span class="st">"retrieved_sources"</span>]:
                    <span class="va">failing_sources</span>[<span class="va">source</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">failing_sources</span>.<span class="fn">get</span>(<span class="va">source</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>) <span class="op">+</span> <span class="nm">1</span>

    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Negative feedback rate: {negative_feedback_count / total_interactions:.1%}"</span>)
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Problem sources: {sorted(failing_sources.items(), key=lambda x: -x[1])[:5]}"</span>)</code></pre></section><section class="section" id="summary"><span class="section-label">Summary</span><h2>Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li><strong>Evaluation Framework:</strong> Measure retrieval, generation, and end-to-end quality separately.</li><li><strong>Retrieval Metrics:</strong> Precision, recall, MRR, NDCG identify when retrieval is the bottleneck.</li><li><strong>Generation Metrics:</strong> Use LLMs to evaluate correctness, relevance, completeness.</li><li><strong>Identify Bottlenecks:</strong> Trace failures to retrieval or generation to guide optimization.</li><li><strong>Systematic Optimization:</strong> Use test sets and metrics to measure improvement.</li><li><strong>Human Evaluation:</strong> Structured rubrics and user feedback provide essential ground truth.</li><li><strong>Production Monitoring:</strong> Log interactions, collect feedback, and identify failure patterns continuously.</li><li><strong>Iteration:</strong> RAG quality improves incrementally. Build monitoring and feedback loops early.</li></ul></div><div class="exercise-box"><h3>Exercise: Full RAG Evaluation</h3><ol><li>Create a dataset of 20 questions with ground truth answers</li><li>For each question, note which documents are relevant</li><li>Run your RAG system and evaluate retrieval metrics</li><li>Evaluate generation quality for top-5 questions</li><li>Identify: Is the bottleneck retrieval or generation?</li><li>Try one optimization (better embedding model or prompt)</li><li>Re-evaluate and measure improvement</li><li>Document what helped and what didn't</li></ol></div><div class="nav-buttons"><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson4" class="nav-btn prev"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M15 18l-6-6 6-6"/></svg> Previous</a></div></div></main><footer><div class="container"><div class="footer-logo"><img src="../../assets/logo_only.png" al